## The 3 Prong Styles

Prong placement on an asscher matters more than on any other shape in the men's series. Here's why.

A round stone has no corners. Prongs can sit anywhere on the girdle — the stone geometry doesn't create weak spots. A princess cut has sharp 90° corners that need V-prong protection. An asscher has 8 sides with 4 beveled corners at 45°. Those corners are beveled — not sharp — so they're more durable than princess. But they still need prong coverage to prevent stone movement.

On a men's ring, the stone takes more impact than on a women's ring. You hit countertops, gym equipment, steering wheels. The prong style you pick determines how well the stone survives that impact for 20 years.

There are 3 prong styles available across the 23 settings in this guide:

---

### Style 1 — Corner-Set 4-Prong

The prong sits directly on each of the 4 beveled corners of the octagon. This is the most secure grip for a men's asscher ring.

Here's why it works: the corner is the structural anchor point of the octagonal outline. When a prong covers the corner, it absorbs impact at the point where the stone geometry changes direction. Force applied from any side travels to the corner — and meets a prong. The stone doesn't rock.

Corner-set also shows the most of the stone's step facets. The 4 sides of the octagon between corners are open. The [8-step lighthouse effect](#the-8-step-octagon-rule) is fully visible from straight above.

**Best for:** Active wear. Daily-wear rings. Gym, outdoor work, hands-on jobs. Any man whose ring takes regular impact.

**Setting recommendation:** The [Structured Solitaire, 14k White Gold at $1,732](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/mens-structured-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-14k-white-gold-item-147399?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) uses an architectural shank with corner-positioned head geometry. Pair with [1ct GIA E-VS1 at $2,464](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28919719?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). Total: $4,196. Corner-set protection on an architectural band.

---

### Style 2 — Side-Set 4-Prong

The prong sits on the long flat sides of the octagon — not at the corners. This is the traditional setting style for asscher on women's rings. On a men's ring, it works for lower-impact wear.

Side-set opens up the stone's corners to view. The octagonal outline is fully visible — the beveled corners are unobstructed. This maximizes the asscher's distinctive geometric silhouette. The stone looks larger because the corners are exposed.

The tradeoff: the beveled corners have no prong coverage. Under impact, the stone can rock slightly within the head. Over years of hard wear, that rocking creates wear on the stone's girdle at the corner positions. It's not an immediate failure — but it's a 10-year risk on a men's ring.

**Best for:** Office wear, formal occasions, light daily use. Remove your ring before gym or manual work.

**Setting recommendation:** The [Ridged Solitaire, 14k White Gold at $1,224](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/mens-ridged-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-14k-white-gold-item-147412?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) uses a ridged shank that echoes the asscher's step-cut geometry. The side-set head lets the octagonal outline show fully. Pair with [1ct GIA F-VS1 at $2,521](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29365844?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). Total: $3,745.

---

### Style 3 — 6-Prong

Six prongs distributed across the octagon's perimeter. Two at the top and bottom. Two on each side. The distribution gives maximum hold with no single stress point.

6-prong is the safest option for the stone itself. If one prong bends or wears, five remain. The stone stays locked. For a lab-grown asscher at 1.5ct+, the face-up increases the stone's leverage against the prongs. 6-prong is the strongest choice.

The visual tradeoff: 6 prongs show more metal over the stone's surface. On a small asscher (1ct), the prongs can feel visually heavy. On a larger stone (1.5ct+), the proportions balance out — more stone, proportionally more prong.

**Best for:** Large lab stones (1.5ct+). Platinum settings — platinum prongs grip tighter but are denser, so 6-prong distributes the weight better. Men who want the strongest possible stone security.

**Setting recommendation:** The [Structured Solitaire, Platinum at $2,424](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/mens-structured-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-platinum-item-147405?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) with 6-prong head. Pair with [1.52ct IGI D-IF lab at $2,795](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27991043?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). Total: $5,219. Platinum 6-prong security on a 1.52ct D-IF lab asscher.

